   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<br><ol><li>I was wondering if it&#39;s possible to choose a model according to AIC with geomorph.</li><li>How do I set fixed/random effects in geomorph?<br></li></ol>Many thanks,<br>Avi<br><br><div title=\"Google Translator Anywhere\" style=\"display:none\"></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "QQAcphfrBAAJ",
   "from": "Mike Collyer",
   "author": "aab55242ff4da6c6",
   "date": "2017-08-15T12:44:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: [geomorph-r-package] AIC with geomorph?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Avi,<div><br></div><div>1. Yes, if you wish to do the math.  Because procD.lm or advanced.procD.lm objects provide residuals, you can calculate the error covariance matrix.  If you can get the error covariance matrix, you can calculate AIC.  The best way to calculate AIC for multivariate data, in my opinion, is presented by, Bedrick, E. J., &amp; Tsai, C. L. (1994). Model selection for multivariate regression in small samples. Biometrics, 226-231.</div><div><br></div><div>Please note that we neither do nor will soon provide AIC or other information criterion values, nor implement an AIC model comparison function in geomorph, as the model selection theory for high-dimensional data (as GM data often are) is not well developed and is perhaps a bit contentious.  We do not wish to inadvertently suggest a theoretical leaning via implementation of tools that have not been well-tested.  I also have some personal concerns with AIC scores (and do not intend to speak for the rest of the geomorph team) with how weights are calculated (data dimensionality has strong influence), how delta-AIC scores should be valued, how practical parameter penalties are, and how small-sample corrections depend on sample size exceeding the number of variables.  I think there is a brighter future in evaluating model performance through MCMC approaches than using an index for ranking models.  But we have a ways to go to realize that future.</div><div><br></div><div>2. I’m not sure what you are asking here.  Is your question how to identify which effects are random, like the function lmer does?  Our functions do not have this capacity, as we only have functions that use least-squares or generalized least squares estimation of coefficients.  One can use nested.update to denote random effects for calculating F values in a limited capacity.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Mike</div><div><br></div><div><br><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Aug 14, 2017, at 2:22 PM, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">netbird....@gmail.com</a> wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div></div>"
